        {
          "id": 1933092,
          "username": "chinaoncall",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/c/258eb7/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-11-06T05:39:16.062Z",
          "cooked": "<p>The honest limitation: a partial refund does not split cleanly down to the line-item level in this CSV. If you need per-SKU refund accuracy for accounting, the export can’t give it to you — that needs the Admin API or an export app that returns refund line items.One thing worth verifying before you build a report on it: check on a single order whether Shopify’s Subtotal is already net of discounts in your export. It usually is, which means subtracting Line item discount from Subtotal double-counts the discount. Test one order by hand — price times quantity minus discount should reconcile to that row’s line total — and you’ll know which way your export behaves.In Excel, the safe pattern is a pivot: rows = Name (order number), values = SUM of Line item discount, and MAX of Discount amount and MAX of Refunded amount. MAX works as a de-duplicating pick here precisely because the value repeats — you get one copy per order instead of N. Then net product revenue per order is line item totals minus line item discounts, with refunds subtracted once at order level.So the rule is not just “don’t add all three” — it’s that only the line-item column can be summed directly. Every order-level column has to be de-duplicated to one value per order first, or your revenue will be wrong in a way that looks plausible enough to ship.Refunded amount is also per order, and repeats the same way.Discount amount is per order. It appears on every row of that order, exactly like Total and Subtotal do. Summing that column multiplies it by the number of items in the order.Line item discount is per row. It applies to that one product line, so summing the column gives you a correct product-discount total.What each one actually is:The reason those three fields look identical is that two of them are order-level and repeat on every line item of the same order. That repetition is what creates the double counting, not the field definitions.</p>",
